import{_ as a,o as t,a as o,d as n}from"./chunks/framework.MWxrmMMs.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Log Analyzer","description":"The Minecraft log analyzer in Stuzhik: the main cause of a crash read from the report itself, steps with buttons checked against the build, crash history and a logs archive for a mod author.","frontmatter":{"description":"The Minecraft log analyzer in Stuzhik: the main cause of a crash read from the report itself, steps with buttons checked against the build, crash history and a logs archive for a mod author.","head":[["meta",{"name":"keywords","content":"minecraft crash analyzer, minecraft crash log analyzer, stuzhik, minecraft log analysis, why does minecraft crash, minecraft crash fix, mixin crash, minecraft crash report"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/log-analyzer.md","filePath":"en/guide/log-analyzer.md"}'),s={name:"en/guide/log-analyzer.md"};function r(i,e,h,l,d,c){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="log-analyzer" tabindex="-1">Log Analyzer <a class="header-anchor" href="#log-analyzer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Log Analyzer&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The analyzer reads the game&#39;s crash report and logs and tells why it crashed and what to do about it. It reads the report&#39;s own structure: the stack trace, the <code>Mixins in Stacktrace</code> section and the mods named there. So it names the culprit by where the code failed, not by which mod the log mentions most. On 93 real crashes from mods&#39; issue trackers it named the right cause in 90 and never blamed the wrong mod. The report is in the launcher&#39;s interface language.</p><h2 id="when-it-runs" tabindex="-1">When it runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-it-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When it runs&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>By itself after a crash.</strong> The launcher reads the crashed game&#39;s log, puts the analysis into «Crash History» and opens the analyzer. Even with the launcher&#39;s window closed, the crash lands in the history</li><li><strong>By hand</strong>: the «Analyze Logs» tab of the build page. «Check again» reads the logs once more</li><li><strong>Without a window</strong>: <code>stuzhik-cli analyze</code> in the <a href="/en/guide/cli.html">console version</a> prints the same analysis</li></ul><h2 id="live-monitoring" tabindex="-1">While you play <a class="header-anchor" href="#live-monitoring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;While you play {#live-monitoring}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>While the game runs, «Live Monitoring» in the window&#39;s title bar follows its log and shows errors and warnings before a crash. A loading error (a KubeJS script, a mixin conflict) opens the analyzer at once.</p><h2 id="solutions" tabindex="-1">The main cause <a class="header-anchor" href="#solutions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The main cause {#solutions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At the top is a card with the main cause: what happened, in which mod, and that mod&#39;s row in the build (which version is installed, or that the build has no such mod). Under it, «What to do» lists steps in order, each with its button:</p><ol><li><strong>Update</strong>: the analyzer checks itself whether there is a newer version for this build and shows which one it will install. When there is nothing newer or the version is pinned, it says so instead of a button that would do nothing</li><li><strong>Disable</strong>: the mod is turned off in the build, «Turn back on» returns it. If the mod is already off, the step says so</li><li><strong>Tell the author</strong>: «Logs archive» packs this run&#39;s logs into a <code>.zip</code>, «Mod page» and «Issue tracker» lead to where to send it</li></ol><p>Every button answers the press at once («Updating…», «Disabling…») and writes the result in the step itself: «updated to …, start the game». An error shows under the step.</p><p>«How this was found» unfolds the report lines it was read from. «Details and solutions online» opens the full description of the problem.</p><h2 id="also-found" tabindex="-1">Also found <a class="header-anchor" href="#also-found" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Also found&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Below the main cause is everything else the analyzer recognised in the log: not enough memory, a broken config, the wrong Java and more. Problems the launcher can fix itself have an action button («Increase the allocated memory», «Reinstall the mod», «Install or update Fabric API» and the like). «Fix All Problems» shows the list of fixes, and «Apply Selected» applies the ones you pick.</p><p>«Not a problem» hides a finding in this build, and later analyses no longer offer it. To bring it back: «Manage dismissed».</p><h2 id="the-«more-actions»-menu-⋯" tabindex="-1">The «More actions» menu (⋯) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-«more-actions»-menu-⋯" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The «More actions» menu (⋯)&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>«A single log file»: analyze one of the build&#39;s logs instead of the last run</li><li>«Open a log from disk…»: any log or crash report, not necessarily from Stuzhik</li><li>«Save the report» and «Open a saved report…»: the analysis to a file and back</li><li>«Log archive for a mod author»: <code>latest.log</code>, the end of <code>debug.log</code>, the latest crash reports and the Stuzhik analysis in one <code>.zip</code></li><li>«Copy as Markdown»: the report to the clipboard, to paste into a chat or a GitHub issue</li><li>«Which fixes are allowed»: the list of fixes the launcher may apply by itself</li></ul><h2 id="crash-history" tabindex="-1">Crash History <a class="header-anchor" href="#crash-history" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Crash History&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The second tab next to «Log analysis». «Statistics»: how many crashes, over the week, which mods are to blame most often and how many were fixed. «History»: each crash with its analysis, «Mark as fixed» marks a fixed one. «Trends»: whether crashes became more or less frequent. «Clear history» removes this build&#39;s records.</p><h2 id="if-nothing-was-found" tabindex="-1">If nothing was found <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-nothing-was-found" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If nothing was found&quot;">​</a></h2><p>«No problems detected» means the logs hold nothing the analyzer knows that gets in the game&#39;s way. Then the «Log archive for a mod author» helps: send it to <a href="https://t.me/stuzhik_launcher" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Telegram</a> or to the mod&#39;s author.</p>',20)])])}const f=a(s,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
